# Sync Devkit

Read the shared install receipt before version comparisons. An absent/invalid receipt means shared migration is pending even when an old compatibility version stamp equals the newest release. Inspect concrete selected provider bindings and installed mode/profile; mixed modes or nonterminal pipelines block without reset.

Resolve the GitHub devkit source owner/repository from the existing entrypoint, preserving its project content. Collect a bounded migration inventory with the shared engine; unknown older layouts receive inventory/conflicts only. Recognized v0.1.48-50 legacy content, including flat instructions and missing stamps, must be reviewed into explicit adaptations. A newer bridge-written stamp requires an exact compatible bridge identity from the pinned bundle's authenticated provenance asset. Different supported legacy versions remain explicit per-provider review evidence. Candidate variants retain original paths, text and hashes; comparison normalizes only known provider substitutions. Genuine divergence has no selected proposal: review all variants into an explicit shared adaptation. Equivalent variants also require review; custom conflicts are never solved by --auto. Bind every detected provider. Preserve provider-local histories and strict counters.

Generate/review adaptive root/context/instruction content and exact hash-backed conflict resolutions. Print the complete plan before applying. Use the concrete Python 3.10+ interpreter and installed helper:

```text
python .mt-agent-devkit/scripts/sync.py --target . --repo <owner/repository> --provider <selected-provider> --mode <installed-mode> --profile <repo|project_root> --adaptations <reviewed-local-json> --output <provider-runtime-plan.json>
```

The helper resolves the release once, pins every read to the resulting commit, reads additive release-object deployment metadata, downloads/verifies every required asset before product writes and delegates to the shared transaction engine. It never fetches `/main` payloads. A missing deployment artifact stops safely; do not reinstall/reset a project or claim certification from an old stamp.

Review every `retire` operation in the printed plan before apply. On a receipt-based install the helper generates a retirement resolution only for a stock file: the path is in the release manifest's retirements, the receipt entry has `adapted: false` and a stock hash, and the file still matches the receipt's rendered hash. Each one has reason `stock retirement in <version>` and is printed as `generated retirement: <path>`; the review lists every generated retirement. Every other retired file stays a conflict. A customised retired file (`adapted: true`) stops with "retired file <path> is customised; port the customization into the replacing skill, then resolve": port the project's customisation into the replacing skill (for the old PO rules files, the matching `po-*` skill; for the old Developer rules files, the matching `dev-*` or common skill; for the old Clean Code rules file, `clean-code`; all under `.mt-agent-devkit/skills/`), then supply an exact reviewed resolution for that path. Legacy 0.1.48-0.1.50 installs have no receipt and keep the manual reviewed resolution for every retired legacy copy. Sync between sprints: a session resumed mid-sprint may still hold retired rules, while a fresh spawn reads the new files.

After authorized review, apply the same saved plan with `deployment.py apply --target . --plan <plan.json>`; avoid resolving the latest release again between plan and apply. Recovery uses the same journal/source identity with `deployment.py resume` or `rollback`. Successful receipt verification is the completion certificate. Report preserved content, exact written/retired paths, conflicts, pending recovery and native evidence limitations. No release is published by sync.
